Solution:
It may help to rewrite f(x) = [\cos (4 x)]^{3}. Use the Chain Rule with inside function u = \cos (4 x) and outside function y = u^{3}. A second application of Chain Rule is used to find \frac{du}{dx}.
\begin{array}{rcl} f'(x) \amp =\amp \displaystyle\frac{dy}{du} \cdot \frac{du}{dx}\\ \amp \amp \\ \amp =\amp (3 u^{2})(-\sin(4 x) \cdot 4) \\ \amp =\amp -12 \cos^{2}(4 x)\sin(4 x) \end{array}
